  "summary": "InvestigateTV's second report on Flock Safety contrasts the company's public claim that its cameras don't track people with its own training webinars and the pan-tilt-zoom Condor camera, which uses AI to follow pedestrians. The article also covers the DeFlock mapping project (nearly 90,000 cameras), a Verizon-linked exposure of ~60-70 cameras streaming without passwords, officer misuse cases, and notes that more than two dozen cities including Denver recently canceled Flock contracts.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "And they quite literally use AI to zoom in and follow you around whether you're a person of interest or not",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Verizon sent us the wrong SIM cards that had a public IP address. We have a lot of sensors in the world that don't have that. Roughly 60-70 devices had a public-facing IP address.",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    },
    {
      "quote": "A feature that you can't turn off is that it's logging everyone else",
      "paragraph_idx": 38
    },
    {
      "quote": "More than two dozen cities recently cancelled their Flock contracts, including Denver, amid concerns over who has access to the company's camera data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 39
    }
  ],
